## 1. Explore before you code

Never write a device test for a screen you have not seen on a device. The design
doc does not contain: the splash delay that eats your first tap, the permission
dialog on Android 13+, the keyboard covering the submit button at 360dp, the back
gesture that leaves the flow instead of dismissing the keyboard.

Drive the flow by hand once. Record what actually happened. Then write the test.

## 2. Route reactive vs. planned before you run

| Reactive (Flash) | Planned (Pro) |
|---|---|
| Observe → act, one screen at a time | Plan with milestones → act → verify each checkpoint |
| ~3–5s per step | ~15–40s per step |
| Known flow, "does this button work" | 100+ step horizons, exploratory, "walk checkout and report" |
| No plan, no final report | Safety check, checkpoint verification, written report |

Start reactive. Move to planned the moment the task needs a claim you must defend.
"An observation does not need a verification graph; a claim does."

## 3. Dynamic-first, coordinate-fallback

Targeting tiers, in order. Do not skip down.

1. **Accessibility id / resource id / content-desc** — stable across redesigns.
2. **Visual / OCR match** — when there is no accessibility tree (Canvas, some Compose, Flutter).
3. **Raw coordinate** — last resort, and a liability.

Coordinates break on rotation, font scaling, and inset changes. Every coordinate
tap carries a comment naming what it was in the previous layout. If you cannot
name it, the test is already broken and you do not know it yet.

## 4. Latency compensation

Transient UI — auto-fading control bars, toasts, spinners — disappears between
turns. Chain taps into one action sequence instead of waiting a model turn per tap.
A blocked action is a **result**: surface it, do not tap past it.

## 5. Prove it, or report it unverified

| Claim | Evidence |
|---|---|
| "The flow works" | End-state screenshot + trace |
| "It crashed" | Logcat stack, not a paraphrase |
| "The button does nothing" | Before + after screenshots, same screen |
| "It's slow" | Per-step latency from the trace |
| "There was a popup" | Screenshot + a decision on whether it blocks |

A blocked action is a finding. Silently retrying until it passes is a fabrication.
Report in the four buckets: succeeded / failed / skipped / unverified.

## 6. Emulator is not device

No real touch latency, no thermal throttling, no OEM skin, no real push path.
An emulator pass is necessary and not sufficient. Run one AVD; two will fight
your other work for RAM.

## 7. Android only

iOS is not covered here. XCUITest or a different tool. Do not promise an iOS
device test from an Android harness.

## 8. Company benchmark claims are claims

A vendor "99% success" on the vendor's own benchmark says nothing about this app's
onboarding, auth, or custom surfaces. Measure this app, do not inherit the number.